  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A sheet of paper won't fit between these hundred-ton stones. Sacsayhuam\u00e1n, above Cusco, at three thousand seven hundred metres.",
        "visual_query": "peruvian andes stone fortress walls",
        "visual_fallback": "andean stonework"
      },
      {
        "narration": "No mortar. Every block a different shape, and every one meets its neighbours exactly. Three terraced walls, each about four hundred metres long.",
        "visual_query": "inca megalithic wall cusco",
        "visual_fallback": "massive stone wall"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The limestone came from quarries kilometres away, over Andean ridges, with no iron tools, no wheels and no draft animals. So how?",
        "visual_query": "andean highland quarry peru",
        "visual_fallback": "mountain quarry"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Cieza de Le\u00f3n wrote in fifteen fifty-three that Pachacuti put twenty thousand men on it, four thousand quarrying, six thousand hauling on leather cables.",
        "visual_query": "sixteenth century spanish manuscript",
        "visual_fallback": "old manuscript"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Best reconstruction: pounded to shape with hammerstones, hauled on ropes and ramps, test-fitted again and again. And the crooked joints are the point. Inca walls settle back into place when the ground shakes.",
        "visual_query": "cusco inca stonework close detail",
        "visual_fallback": "carved stone joints"
      }
    ],
